  <ferc:DisclosureOfChangesInOfficersDirectorsMajorSecurityHoldersAndVotingPowersOfTheRespondentTextBlock id="f-21" contextRef="c-1">&lt;div style="padding-left:18pt;text-indent:-18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;13. The following changes in officers occurred during the year ended December 31, 2025:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:18pt;text-indent:-18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;Officer resignations:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt;text-indent:-36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;&#8226;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%;padding-left:32.85pt"&gt;Natalie F. Smith, Vice President&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt;text-indent:-36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;&#8226;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%;padding-left:32.85pt"&gt;Michael H. Dunne, Vice President&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:18pt;text-indent:-22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;Newly Appointed Officers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt;text-indent:-36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;&#8226;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%;padding-left:32.85pt"&gt;Matthew R. Geoffroy, Assistant Treasurer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt;text-indent:-36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;&#8226;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%;padding-left:32.85pt"&gt;Matt Pawlowski, Vice President&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfChangesInOfficersDirectorsMajorSecurityHoldersAndVotingPowersOfTheRespondentTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfSignificantEventThatRespondentParticipatesInACashManagementProgramsTextBlock id="f-22" contextRef="c-1">&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;14. None&lt;/span&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfSignificantEventThatRespondentParticipatesInACashManagementProgramsTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfImportantDisclosuresRegardingTheFinancialStatementsTextBlock id="f-23" contextRef="c-1">&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Introduction&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The accompanying financial statements were prepared in accordance with the accounting requirements of the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission ("FERC") as set forth in its applicable Uniform System of Accounts and published accounting releases, which is a comprehensive basis of accounting that varies from generally accepted accounting principles in the United States of America ("GAAP"). These requirements differ from GAAP related to (1) the presentation of regulatory debits and credits (2) the presentation of deferred income taxes (3) the presentation of regulatory assets and liabilities (4) treatment and presentation of goodwill (5) removal costs and (6) the presentation of debt issuance costs. Accordingly, certain footnotes are not prepared on the same basis as the financial statements presented herein. The information below is presented in millions unless otherwise stated. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;NOTE 1 - ORGANIZATION AND BUSINESS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;GridLiance West LLC ("GLW", &#8220;the Company&#8221;), formed in 2014, is a wholly-owned subsidiary of GridLiance Western Holdings LLC (&#8220;GWH&#8221;). GWH is a wholly-owned indirect subsidiary of NextEra Energy Transmission, LLC, ("NEET"), which in turn is a wholly-owned subsidiary of NextEra Energy Infrastructure, LLC ("NEEI"). NEEI is a wholly-owned subsidiary of NextEra Energy Capital Holdings, Inc. ("NEECH"), which in turn is a wholly-owned subsidiary of NextEra Energy, Inc., a company listed on the New York Stock Exchange.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The Company, a single member limited liability company organized under the laws of Delaware, is a rate regulated transmission provider in California. Its purpose is to collaborate with rural electric cooperatives, municipal utilities, joint action agencies and others to plan for the future of the grid within California Independent System Operator Corp. (&#8220;CAISO&#8221;) located in the United States. The Company invests in necessary electric infrastructure and implements strategies to improve system reliability and resiliency and reduce overall costs to customers.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;NOTE 2 - SIGNIFICANT ACCOUNTING POLICIES&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Use of Estimates&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; - The preparation of financial statements requires management to make estimates and assumptions that affect the reported amounts of assets and liabilities and disclosure of contingent assets and liabilities at the date of the financial statements and the reported amounts of revenues and expenses during the reporting period. Actual results could differ from those estimates.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Regulatory Accounting - &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;As a public utility operating in California, the Company is within the jurisdiction of the California Public Utilities Commission ("CPUC") with respect to operational performance, use and disposition of utility property, siting of new utility assets, and other CPUC regulations and general orders. However, because the Company is engaged exclusively in providing the wholesale transmission of electricity in interstate commerce, the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission ("FERC") has sole jurisdiction to regulate and approve the Company&#8217;s annual Transmission Revenue Requirement ("TRR") as set forth in its Transmission Owner Tariff. The TRR is based on the Company&#8217;s costs of service and is intended to allow the Company to recover costs prudently incurred in the development, financing, construction, operation and maintenance of the Project including a return on capital invested in the Project. The Company&#8217;s TRR is paid by the CAISO pursuant to a Participating Transmission Owner ("PTO") agreement under which the Company has a right to share in a portion of the total transmission access charge the CAISO levies on all entities that withdraw energy from the CAISO-controlled transmission grid. Such TRR is generally paid without regard to Project usage or availability.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;As a regulated utility, the Company records assets and liabilities that result from the regulated ratemaking process that would otherwise not be recorded under GAAP for non-regulated companies. Regulatory assets represent costs that will be included as a component of future tariff rates and regulatory liabilities represent amounts that have been collected in current rates to recover costs that are expected to be incurred, or refunded to customers, in future periods. In the prior year, footnotes were prepared consistent with the Company's GAAP financial statements. In the current year, both periods are presented consistent with how balances and transactions are presented under the Uniform System of Accounts.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;When regulatory assets are probable of recovery through regulated rates, the Company records them as assets on the balance sheet. Once a regulatory asset is recorded, the Company reviews the probability of recovery at each balance sheet date, considering the impact of any changes or events during the period. If the likelihood of future recovery of any regulatory asset becomes less than probable, the Company will write off that regulatory asset as a charge against income.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;See Note 3 &#8211; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Regulatory Matter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;s for additional information. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Cash and Cash Equivalents&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; - All highly liquid investments with a maturity at the date of purchase of three months or less are considered cash. Cash for short-term working capital needs is held in financial institutions.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Accounts Receivable &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;- Receivables are associated with revenues earned from contracts with customers consisting of both billed and unbilled amounts. Receivables represent unconditional rights to consideration and reflect the differences in timing of revenue recognition and cash collections. If any of the customer receivable balances should be deemed uncollectible, it could have a material adverse effect on the Company's results of operations and financial condition. However, management does not believe significant credit risk existed at December 31, 2025 and 2024. Accounts receivable balances would be adjusted for any write-offs and any estimated allowance for doubtful accounts, which is calculated on the basis of the expected credit losses. The allowance for doubtful accounts is reviewed periodically, considering historical and current information such as average write-offs to estimate the expected credit losses. There was no allowance for doubtful accounts recorded as of December 31, 2025 or 2024, respectively.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Revenue Recognition - The Company has one customer, the CAISO, who accounts for 100% of the Company&#8217;s transmission revenue. The Company recognizes revenue on a straight-line basis on its annual Transmission Revenue Requirement ("TRR") as approved by the FERC and collects cash on a weekly basis from the CAISO. When actual cash receipts are different than the TRR, a Transmission Revenue Balancing Account ("TRBA") is used to accumulate these timing differences. The Company prepares a Transmission Revenue Balancing Account Adjustment ("TRBAA") filing with the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission ("FERC") on an annual basis based on the timing difference balance at September 30.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Prepaid Expenses and Other Current Assets - &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Prepaid expenses and other current assets consist primarily of deposits and prepaid amounts. Prepaid amounts expected to be used within one year are classified as current. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Property, Plant and Equipment, Net&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; - Property, plant and equipment, net consists primarily of transmission assets. Property, plant and equipment are recorded at cost and depreciated on a straight-line average remaining life basis. Accumulated depreciation consists of the cost of units of property retired less estimated net salvage value. Maintenance and repairs of property as well as replacements and renewals of items determined to be less than units of property are charged to operations and maintenance expenses. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Construction work in progress includes construction materials, progress payments on major equipment contracts, third-party engineering costs and other costs directly associated with the construction of various projects. Upon completion of the projects, these costs are transferred to property, plant and equipment in service.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Depreciation of the Company's transmission assets is provided on a straight-line basis, primarily over its average remaining useful life as approved by the FERC.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;See Note 4 - &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Property, Plant and Equipment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; for additional information. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Impairment of Long-Lived Assets&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; - Long-lived assets that are held and used are reviewed for impairment whenever events or changes in circumstances indicate carrying values may not be recoverable. As of December 31, 2025 and 2024, the Company concluded no impairment adjustments were necessary.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;AFUDC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; - Allowance for funds used during construction ("AFUDC") represents the estimated costs of borrowed and equity funds used to finance regulated plant additions before they go into service and is capitalized as part of the cost of construction. AFUDC is recoverable through rates over the life of the related asset once that asset is placed in service. AFUDC related to the cost of borrowed funds is recorded as a reduction to interest expense. AFUDC related to the cost of equity funds is recorded as a component of other income. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Debt Issuance Costs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; - Debt issuance costs are costs incurred and capitalized in connection with obtaining financing and are amortized over the expected term of the debt as interest expense using a method which approximates the effective-interest method. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt;See Note 5 &#8211; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt;Debt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt; for additional information. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Income Taxes -&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; Deferred income taxes are provided on all significant temporary differences between the financial statement and tax basis of assets or liabilities. The significant temporary differences result primarily from property basis differences caused by differences in book and tax depreciation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Included in regulatory assets on the Company's balance sheets is the revenue equivalent of the difference in accumulated deferred income taxes computed under accounting rules, as compared to regulatory accounting rules. Included within regulatory liabilities on the Company's balance sheets is the impact of tax reform, as the federal tax rate changed from 35% to 21% in 2018. The resulting excess deferred taxes will be returned to rate payers in the future.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;Leases - &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;The Company determines if an arrangement is a lease at inception. The Company recognizes a right-of-use (&#8220;ROU&#8221;) asset and a lease liability for operating leases by recognizing and measuring leases at the commencement date based on the present value of lease payments over the lease term. The Company has elected not to apply the recognition requirements to short-term leases and not to separate non-lease components from associated lease components for substantially all classes of underlying assets. Operating lease expense is included in operations and maintenance ("O&amp;amp;M") expense in the statements of operations.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;NOTE 3 - REGULATORY MATTERS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Formula Rate Filings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The Company principally generates revenue through transmitting electricity to its customers. Substantially all of the Company&#8217;s material revenue streams consist of FERC-approved, tariff-based revenue and is settled by CAISO. Under the Company&#8217;s formula rate, all revenue credits, including revenue related to pole attachment agreements, are deducted from the gross revenue requirement to calculate the net revenue requirement for the year. The Company recognizes revenue from the transmission of electricity proportionally throughout the year based on the FERC-approved TRR as calculated prior to the beginning of each year (the &#8220;Rate Year&#8221;) using the FERC-approved formula rate template. CAISO bills load-serving customers for actual usage based on the TRR and estimated load. Differences between the billed rates and TRR are recorded to the TRBA. The TRBA, with an annual adjustment filed with FERC, ensures that all transmission revenue credits and adjustments for any over- or under-recovery of the annual TRR flow through to transmission customers. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Following a Public Letter Ruling issued by the Internal Revenue Service in April 2017, FERC initiated a proceeding into the methodology used by the Company (and various other public utilities) for calculating accumulated deferred income taxes (&#8220;ADIT&#8221;) balances in its projected test year and annual true-up calculations for the transmission formula rates. Subsequently, FERC issued an order in December 2018 directing the Company to revise its formula rate template to adopt FERC&#8217;s revised methodology to calculate ADIT balances in its transmission formula rates. In March 2019, the FERC approved the Company&#8217;s revisions to its formula rate template to adopt the FERC&#8217;s proposed ADIT methodology. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Following the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017 (&#8220;TCJA&#8221;), FERC issued an order in November 2019 directing utilities to revise their formula rate templates to revise their calculation of ADIT to account for the tax rate changes caused by the TCJA. The Company submitted its filing in April 2020, which is still pending Commission approval as of the filing date.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On January 29, 2019, FERC instituted a proceeding pursuant to Federal Power Act section 206 to examine the Company&#8217;s recovery of an income tax allowance in its formula rate template. The Commission found that the Company&#8217;s recovery of an income tax allowance may result in an unjust double recovery of income taxes as described in the D.C. Circuit&#8217;s United Airlines decision and FERC&#8217;s Revised Policy Statement on Treatment of Income Taxes. The Commission set this issue for hearing and settlement proceedings. On July 29, 2019, the Company filed an offer of settlement to resolve the income tax allowance issue. As part of that offer of settlement, the Company&#8217;s existing income tax allowance was set at a negotiated percentage of 72.13% through June 30, 2020 and then set at 53.29% effective July 1, 2020. Further, the Company&#8217;s base ROE of 9.6% was subject to a rate moratorium until July 1, 2020, such that no settling party could request any changes to the base ROE to become effective prior to the expiration of the moratorium. On September 5, 2019, the Settlement Judge certified the uncontested settlement for FERC&#8217;s approval, which the Commission approved on December 19, 2019. On February 19 and April 29, 2020, the Company filed revised tariff sheets to reflect the Income Tax Allowance calculations, and FERC issued letter orders approving them on October 13, 2021.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Pursuant to the Income Tax Settlement referenced above, the Company is obligated to adjust the mechanism in its formula rate template to recover its income tax allowance if an upstream change in control materially impacts the underlying basis for the settled income tax allowance percentages. NEET&#8217;s acquisition of 100% of the upstream ownership interests in the Company satisfies this condition. As such, on April 1, 2021, the Company has revised its &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;formula rate template to allow for 100% recovery of the Company&#8217;s income tax allowance. On May 20, 2021, FERC approved the Company's Filing, effective May 31, 2021.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Other Proceedings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On July 19, 2023, the Company submitted a request with FERC to recover 100% of all prudently incurred costs associated with its investment in two projects approved by the CAISO Board of Directors on May 18, 2023, the GLW/VEA Area Upgrades and the Beatty 230 kV Project, in the event that either project is ultimately abandoned for reasons outside of the Company's control. The Commission granted the Company's request on August 30, 2023. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On August 3, 2023, the Company submitted an application pursuant to section 204 of the Federal Power Act requesting authorization to issues long-term debt securities as well as waiver from the Commission&#8217;s competitive bidding and negotiated placement requirements. The Commission issued an order authorizing the issuance of such securities on September 29, 2023.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On April 29, 2024, the Company submitted a request with FERC for incentive rate treatment pursuant to Order No. 679, for its investment in the GLW/VEA Area Upgrades ("the Project"). Specifically, the Company requested authorization to include in rate base 100% of prudently incurred Construction Work in Progress ("CWIP") related to the Project&#8217;s development. The Commission granted the Company's request in Docket No. ER24-1865-000 on June 28, 2024 effective June 29, 2024. Since CWIP in rate base during the construction period is considered an acceleration of a return on the allowance for funds used during construction, the Company believes that prior to the Project going into service, CWIP in rate base should be recorded as an Alternative Revenue Program (&#8220;ARP&#8221;) as an increase to a regulatory asset with the income statement offset being revenue from regulatory mechanisms. As of December 31, 2024, the Company has recognized $21.6 million related to this program.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;In the fourth quarter 2025, the Company submitted applications to the Public Utility Commission of Nevada ("PUCN") under the Utility Environmental Protection Act ("UEPA") for permits to construct 230 kV substation improvements and associated facilities to interconnect several generation projects to the existing Trout Canyon Substation. GLW expects a decision on these UEPA applications in the first quarter of 2026.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Regulatory Assets and Liabilities&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The Company's regulatory assets and liabilities are as follows:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:10pt;text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;table style="border-collapse:collapse;display:inline-table;margin-bottom:5pt;vertical-align:text-bottom;width:100.000%"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:50.566%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:6.677%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:18.900%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.355%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:18.902%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="9" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;December 31,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:100%"&gt;Regulatory Assets&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;2025&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;2024&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt